<setup>

  <selectFromSequence name="p" from="-3" to="-1" step="1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="q" from="2" to="4" step="1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="r" from="5" to="7" step="1"/>
  
  <function name="f">$p*x^2 + $q*x + $r</function>
  <function name="fP">2*$p*x + $q</function>
  <function name="fPP">2*$p</function>
  <function name="fPPP">0</function>
  <function name="fP4">0</function>
  
  <math name="c0">$$f($a)</math>
  <math name="c1">$$fP($a)</math>
  <math name="c2">$$fPP($a)/2</math>
  <math name="c3">$$fPPP($a)/3!</math>
  <math name="c4">$$fP4($a)/4!</math>
  
<!--
  <selectFromSequence name="c0" from="-2" to="2" step="1" exclude="0"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="c1" from="-3" to="3" step="1" exclude="0 $c0"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="c2" from="-3" to="3" step="1" exclude="0 $c0 $c1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="c3" from="-3" to="3" step="1" exclude="0 $c0 $c1 $c2"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="c4" from="-3" to="3" step="1" exclude="0 $c0 $c1 $c2 $c3"/>
-->
  
  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="2" to="4" step="1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="pm" from="-1" to="1" step="2"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="k" from="0.3" to="0.5" step="0.1"/>

  <math simplify name="h">$a + $pm*$k</math>
  
  <function name="T4">$c0 + $c1*(x-$a) + $c2*(x-$a)^2 + $c3*(x-$a)^3 + $c4*(x-$a)^4</function>
  <function name="T4Symb">c_0 + c_1*(x-$a) + c_2*(x-$a)^2 + c_3*(x-$a)^3 + c_4*(x-$a)^4</function>
  
  <function name="T3">$c0 + $c1*(x-$a) + $c2*(x-$a)^2 + $c3*(x-$a)^3</function>
  <function name="T3Symb">c_0 + c_1*(x-$a) + c_2*(x-$a)^2 + c_3*(x-$a)^3</function>

  <function name="T2">$c0 + $c1*(x-$a) + $c2*(x-$a)^2</function>
  <function name="T2Symb">c_0 + c_1*(x-$a) + c_2*(x-$a)^2</function>

  <function name="T1">$c0 + $c1*(x-$a)</function>
  <function name="T1Symb">c_0 + c_1*(x-$a)</function>
  
</setup>

<p>
  Consider the quadratic function <m>f(x) = <math simplify>$p x^2 + $q x + $r</math></m>.  Let's find the quadratic Taylor polynomial approximation of <m>f</m> centered at <m>a = $a</m>.
</p>

<!--
<p>
  <m>pm = $pm</m>, <m>k = $k</m>
</p>
-->

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        Find all of the needed derivatives of <m>f(x) = <math simplify>$p x^2 + $q x + $r</math></m> as well as their respective values at <m>a = $a</m>.  Report your results for <m>f''(x)</m> and <m>f''($a)</m> below.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>f''(x) = </m><answer>$fPP</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>f''($a) = </m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>$$fPP($a)</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
          Find and state each of the first two Taylor polynomials of <m>f</m> that are centered at <m>a = $a</m>: <m>T_1(x)</m> and <m>T_2(x)</m>.  
      </p>    
      <p>
        <m>T_1(x) = </m><answer name="T1Entered" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>$T1</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>T_2(x) = </m><answer name="T2Entered" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>$T2</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Use <m>T_2(x)</m> to estimate the value of <m>f($h)</m>.  Report your result to an accuracy of at least <m>0.000001</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>f($h) \approx T_2(<math simplify>$h</math>) = </m><answer name="fEst" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>$$T4($h)</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        Note that since <m>f(x)</m> is quadratic, its value is straightforward to compute for any <m>x</m>.  Find <m>f($h)</m> exactly and report its result below.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>f($h) = </m><answer name="fIs" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>$$f($h)</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Use a computational device to plot both <m>f(x)</m> and <m>T_2(x)</m> on the same coordinate axes.  Complete the following sentence to summarize what you notice.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since <m>f(x)</m> is itself a <choiceInput inline name="ans1">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>linear</choice>
          <choice>quadratic</choice>
          <choice>exponential</choice>
        </choiceInput>
          function, its degree <m>2</m> Taylor polynomial approximation <m>T_2(x)</m> will be <choiceInput inline name="ans2">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>different from <m>f(x)</m> except at <m>a = $a</m></choice>
          <choice>identical to <m>f(x)</m> for every value of <m>x</m></choice>
        </choiceInput> because knowing the height, slope, and concavity of any quadratic function at a single value of <m>x</m> determines the function uniquely.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><award><when>
          $ans1.selectedIndices=3 and $ans2.selectedIndices=3
        </when></award></answer>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>